Subject: [PATCH] Guest RAM 256 MB (N7 step 1): the most the EE's address map allows, the 32 MB layout
 kept behind PS2X_GUEST_RAM_MB=32

Main RAM is 256 MB (PS2_RAM_SIZE): the I/O space starts at 0x10000000, the uncached-accelerated mirror at
0x30000000 needs bit 28 free and DMA tags address 28 bits, so 256 MB is the ceiling. The generated code's inline
RAM path derives from PS2_RAM_SIZE, so every generated file recompiles (none is regenerated).

How much of it a game gets is the guest layout's (PS2Runtime::guestLayout, read once from PS2X_GUEST_RAM_MB):
- 32: the PS2's layout as before - the heap up to 0x01FA8000, the port's added data from there to the main
  thread's stack at 0x01FE0000, GetMemorySize 32 MB.
- anything else (the default): 256 MB - the heap's first block up to the main thread's stack (0x01FE0000, as on
  hardware), a second block from 0x02000000 to the port's added data (its 1 MB at the top of RAM),
  GetMemorySize 256 MB. A game that probes malloc for its largest block gets the second one.

ps2xRuntime:
- ps2_memory.h: PS2_RAM_SIZE 256 MB.
- ps2_runtime.h/.cpp: GuestLayout + guestLayout(); the heap's hard limit from it (was the 0x01FA8000 constant);
  resetGuestHeapLocked adds the second block; guestHeapLargestFree() (a game-side probe override's answer);
  the initial stack pointer where the 32 MB ended (0x02000000 - 16), not the top of the larger RAM.
- Kernel/Syscalls/System.cpp: SetupHeap/EndOfHeap's default end = the layout's first block; GetMemorySize = the
  layout's RAM; SetupThread's "top of RAM" stack (stack -1) at 0x02000000.
- ps2_savestate.cpp/.h: the layout's RAM size is part of the state's layout check; a state made with another RAM
  size is refused saying so (they need making again). load() says whether a failure came before anything was
  overwritten (`untouched`: a missing or unreadable file, another build's or RAM size's state). Main RAM is stored
  by 4 KB pages: runs of all-zero pages and of pages filled with one word as a count, the rest raw - most of the
  256 MB is never written, so a state is about the size of the 32 MB ones, and quicker to save and load. A state
  with the whole RAM as one blob (made before) still loads; PS2X_STATE_RAW=1 writes that form (an A/B). The save
  and load times (and the saved size) in the [state] lines.
- Kernel/EeScheduler.cpp, runtime/ee_scheduler.h: such a refused load no longer stops the runtime - the game runs on
  as it was (an in-game load, F9) or boots fresh (a load at start-up, PS2X_LOAD_STATE); the refusal is counted
  (refusedStateLoadCount) with its file and reason (lastRefusedStateLoad). A failure after the first section was
  applied still stops it (the machine is half restored).
- ps2_runtime.cpp: the refusal across the bottom of the window for 10 s ("Save state not loaded - <file>: <reason>"),
  drawn as the FPS counter is, whichever overlay is on; a "[state] on screen" line in the log when it is.

